RECEIPT 01 — THE MARQUIS INCIDENT

Marquis's federal complaint states that on August 14, 2025, a third-party threat actor executed a ransomware attack, gained unauthorized access to Marquis's network and accessed client information.

Marquis says the affected information included PII belonging to customers of some financial-institution clients.

Classification

E5 — PRIMARY COURT FILING

The incident itself is not disputed.

RECEIPT 02 — MARQUIS SAYS ITS FIREWALLS WERE CURRENT

This makes the case technically interesting.

Marquis alleges that before the attack:

its SonicWall firewalls were current
administrative accounts had MFA
complex passwords were used
administrative interfaces were restricted through allow lists
Active Directory integration was configured
gateway security features were enabled
content filtering was used
botnet filtering was used
geo-blocking was configured

That does not prove the environment was perfectly secured.

It does establish Marquis's litigation position:

The attacker did not simply succeed because Marquis forgot to turn on MFA or left a known unpatched firewall exposed.

RECEIPT 03 — SONICWALL'S CLOUD BACKUP SERVICE WAS BREACHED

This is independently confirmed.

SonicWall acknowledged unauthorized access to customer firewall configuration backup files stored in its MySonicWall cloud environment.

Those backups contained:

firewall configuration information
encrypted credentials
configuration metadata

SonicWall warned possession of the material increased the risk of targeted attacks.

Classification

E5 — VENDOR CONFIRMED

RECEIPT 04 — 5% BECAME 100%

This may be one of the strongest SecurityReceipts timelines of the entire 2026 archive.

Initial SonicWall disclosure:

<5% OF FIREWALL INSTALL BASE

Later determination:

ALL CUSTOMERS
WHO USED THE CLOUD BACKUP SERVICE

That doesn't necessarily mean SonicWall intentionally minimized the incident.

Incident scope frequently expands during forensic investigations.

But InfinityGrid should preserve the timeline.

Receipt Timeline
SEPTEMBER 2025
SonicWall detects suspicious cloud-backup activity
        ↓
Initial assessment:
fewer than 5% of firewall install base affected
        ↓
Mandiant-supported investigation
        ↓
OCTOBER 2025
Scope revised:
ALL cloud-backup customers affected

That is exactly why SecurityReceipts exists.

Don't overwrite the first statement when the second appears.

Archive both.

RECEIPT 05 — WHAT WAS INSIDE THE BACKUPS?

Firewall backups aren't ordinary documents.

Depending upon configuration and device generation, these files can expose information regarding:

firewall rules
routing
network architecture
VPN configuration
user/group information
authentication configuration
service settings
DNS configuration
certificates
enabled services
encrypted credentials
secrets
access-control structures

Even if individual secrets remain encrypted, configuration intelligence has offensive value.

Think:

ATTACKER BEFORE BACKUP THEFT

Unknown:
- firewall model
- VPN topology
- exposed services
- internal ranges
- authentication configuration
- policy structure

        ↓

ATTACKER AFTER BACKUP THEFT

Potentially knows:
- device configuration
- service exposure
- VPN design
- authentication relationships
- routing
- users/groups
- policy structure
- where secrets exist

The attacker's reconnaissance burden collapses.

RECEIPT 06 — SONICWALL'S OWN WARNING

SonicWall did not say the stolen files were harmless.

It explicitly warned that possession of the backups could increase the risk of targeted attacks.

This distinction matters.

SonicWall can legitimately dispute:

“Our incident definitely caused Marquis.”

while simultaneously acknowledging:

“The stolen material created additional risk to affected customers.”

Both statements can be true.

